{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/1524397-hunters-pass-beasts-fail","id":1524397,"title":"Hunters Pass, Beasts Fail?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Beast: The Primordial"],"posts":[{"date":"2023-10-22T13:32:40-07:00","id":1524397,"title":"Hunters Pass, Beasts Fail?","content":"So there I was, running late with my day in general, trying to get things I needed done today, when all of a sudden a thought intruded and demanded I give attention, so I'm doing that. Let's start by elaborating on that provocative title!<br /> <br /> There's been an argument, not as old as others for Beast, but kind of old, in that part of what makes Beast: the Primordial a turn off for people is that their Satiation of Hunger has no fantastic filter-the actions that a Beast commits to are all real world shocks and horrors, from throwing a brick through a window or beating someone up to destroying a person's home or ripping out a hamstring or two.<br /> <br /> Now this can be a understandable issue-a lot of that can be hard to think about, nevermind viscerally describing or hearing it described to you-but we're in a horror game, where other monsters can and do rip the limbs off of people and beat them up with it, manipulate and gaslight others into doing and thinking what they want them to think and do, etc., etc. But to be fair on that front, and going into the complaint, some of that exists in the realm of cartoonish excess or fantastical implementation. While I think it's delusional to imagine players characters never just go &quot;No, that didn't happen it, you imagined it&quot; in addition to their emotion manipulating powers or what not, you can conceivably argue that all of the horrific action of the game can feasibly be only performed through the side of the supernatural angle.<br /> <br /> But then you get to Hunter.<br /> <br /> Hunter, as a gameline, is predominantly not a game where the violence of it's forms is expressed through a supernatural veneer. Sure, the Conspiracies have their Endowments, and nothing says that hunters can't also have supernatural merits, but the game primarily markets itself where you deal with someone you don't approve of by running their face through a chainsaw blade. There is a lot of sense of the mundanity in the physicality and psychology being used to harm, manipulate, denigrate, and otherwise impose unwanted conditions upon other people. If your average hunter wants to make someone do something they don't want to do, they probably do it through lies, deception, using a person's perception and memory against them, and even drug and/or other substance abuse.<br /> <br /> THe immediate counter is that in these cases, the scenario is flipped-the actions may be real world transgressions, but the victims are fantastical entities, and are often the bad guys-but that falls short in a couple ways for me. Humans often find ways to be cast as antagonists, even villains, in other games readily enough (hell, Hunter regularly fills in for those roles alone, but as a quick rundown of other options, Forsaken has humans as Prey and have things like RD-13 and Lupus Et Fidelis in their portfolio, Prometheans regularly have humanity as opponents even when they aren't alchemists, demiurges, or clone-scientists, changelings have every family and friend member who gets onto the fact that there is an alien trying to pass among them, Sin-eaters often have humans intruding on their quest to resolve their Burdens even without necromancers and Eaters of the Dead, the Arisen have Last Dynasty International, Demons have the Deva Corporation, and Deviant's main opponents are Baselines most of the time, and even the Awakened can find the structures of humanity and the people who support, engineer and manipulate them as being in the way of their own work), and just as often the humans you get cast against are victims you probably don't have context on as much as they are monsters in their own right. For every Loyalist trying to trick a mortal into the Hedge there is, there's a Lost desperately trying to get someone important to them to finally get what's going on-and hunters don't have a privilege to see a difference between the two just because we as players know the difference.<br /> <br /> The only other major difference I can see at the moment is that Hunter's are, directly or indirectly, working to such an end that is ostensibly altruistic-reclaim the night for humanity, however that's interpreted-where as Beast's are all self-interested and self-involved, but that equally ignores both that hunters often chase that altruistic end, of saying No More to monstrous predation and exploitation, because they are chasing an emotional fix to having been hurt or violated, that their motives can be very selfish and the good they are doing is a perk or cover up for it, and likewise that a Beast can bend their Hungers towards ends with an eye for improving their community and the lives in it. They can go at it from different directions, but it doesn't take much to see how both avarice and altruism can work in either of the splats in regards to what they do, nevermind the ways they can commit to both with it.<br /> <br /> Sooooooooooooooooooooooooooo<br /> <br /> Square the circle-how is a Hunter's Vigil meaningfully different from a Beast's Hunger in terms of the violence it commits?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-22T14:33:47-07:00","id":1524399,"title":"","content":"Hunters aren't obligate predators; Beasts are. When a Hunter resorts to violence, it's a deliberate decision, and usually a response to violence from a monster. When a Beast turns violent, it's because he needs to fill his Satiety meter.<br /> <br /> The compacts and conspiracies who <i>don't</i> hunt monsters because they're threats - Ashwood Abbey, who do it for fun; the Cheiron Group, who do it for money - get the same disgusted response as Beasts. The thought &quot;I do this only because my targets deserve to suffer&quot; is apparently necessary, when the supernatural veneer is absent.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":7}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1133-michael-brazier","name":"Michael Brazier","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"161"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-22T17:47:17-07:00","id":1524407,"title":"","content":"There are important differences, typically in a game where the Forsaken encounter RD-13 they may kill humans but they won't actively torment them. Hunters, likewise, don't typically play with their food, and if they stray too much into doing bad things to good people well they have a morality stat and slasherdom (and NPC status) awaits.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27618-shadowcatx","name":"ShadowcatX","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27618_2.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"560"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-22T21:51:12-07:00","id":1524410,"title":"","content":"You can play a Hunter whose only interactions with the supernatural are inquisitive and/or warding - Network Zero dudes helping ghosts move on, Wildlife Solutions response teams isolating and rehabilitating cryptids and chimera, even TF:V teams living out episodes of Eureka/Warehouse 13. Like said, playing a hunter whose proclivities turn your stomach is a deliberate choice, both in and out of character.<br /> <br /> Beast, on the other hand, are <i>obligated</i> to be repugnant. At best, you can go the &quot;family meal&quot; mechanic, or play the whole thing up as a personal tortured drama. But you know what? That &quot;woe is me, I am so monstrous and must harm others to survive!&quot; tripe is barely tolerable in Vampire. In Beast, -the core book at least, I've not read the rest- the narrative oozes an excuse plot to torture expies of people IRL the player doesn't like.<br /> <br /> And actually, that's the thing that makes me unable to treat Beast like I would any other splat, including Hunter. The rest all have a... theme? Plot?<br /> You go into a Mage game, you generally expect to be solving mysteries.<br /> Werewolf? Keep the local Shadow in order, be it from Hosts, Pure, Magath, Idagam, etc.<br /> Sin-Eater? Help ghosts, explore the Underworld.<br /> Vampire? Court politics.<br /> Changeling? Ditto, with a side of avoiding the Fae and possibly reclaiming your old life. <br /> Mummy? On a mission from Gods.<br /> Demon? Flee the God-Machine, make Cover, Interlocks, build Hell, <br /> Promethean? Become human.<br /> Deviant? Fight/flee the Web, master your scars.<br /> Hunter? Combat that which goes bump in the night.<br /> But Beast? Going off the core book alone -and that's all that most people are going to look at, IMO- there very little to the game beyond &quot;find target, torture them, possible kill them, repeat&quot;. The whole gameplay loop seems to center around hurting people, and -again, based on Core- treats anyone who fights against that as, at best, misguided.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\">Monkish Asexual.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1490057-cauthon-s-homebrew-hub#post1490057\" target=\"_blank\">My homebrew hub</a><br /> <br /> Mostly checked out, there's an uncomfortable amount of condescension in the old guard.</div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":8},{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27258-cauthon","name":"Cauthon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27258_4.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1780"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-23T00:34:56-07:00","id":1524411,"title":"","content":"'Meaningful' difference. I guess the differences usually brushed away are meaningful after all? Beauty is skin deep, but the skin is fricking big and important. I mean, people want to be vindicated. Hunters in Hunter often are. Beasts in Beast rarely are.<br /> <br /> Actually, on surface it feels the opposite - Hunter doesn't particularly hide that you're playing violent vigilantes wrapped up in potential conspiracy theory material, while Beast does its best to assure that you are fighting for your own story, etc. I blame unintended reverse psychology at work here.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>MtAw Homebrew:<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1051456-even-more-legacies-updated-to-2e#post1051456\" target=\"_blank\">Even more Legacies, updated to 2E</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1443819-new-2e-legacies-expanded\" target=\"_blank\">New 2E Legacies, expanded</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12619-21c-hermit","name":"21C Hermit","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12619_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3489"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-23T01:39:10-07:00","id":1524415,"title":"","content":"Ultimately, I don't think the &quot;no fantasy filter&quot; issue is rational, so expecting it to apply evenly is going to work even with people considering things with maximal charity and good faith intention.<br /> <br /> There are some people that don't mind clowns. There are other people that hate clowns and all clowns are a hard nope for them. But there are also people where they need to take each character on a case-by-case basis because what exactly makes them dislike clowns is too variable to come up with a simple solution (and some of them will avoid clowns because the time and energy of separating out the ones they can enjoy and the creep-inducing ones isn't worth the effort). While people might be able to articulate specific reasons clowns creep them out; it's ultimately an emotional response that can't be addressed by pointing to how androids usually get a pass (unless the creators are purposefully pushing the Uncanny Valley as hard as possible).<br /> <br /> While I find the... uncritical... analysis of Beast endless frustrating, the illogical application of the fantasy-filter is something I can sympathize with because it isn't about addressing a purely logical issue. If someone wants to sit down and really dig into why they kinda let a bunch of other game-lines do stuff, but hold Beast to a higher standard? That's a conversation I think is worth engaging in, but I think it's ultimately a personal one. Getting into an Internet debate on how people give the Joker the Clown Prince of Crime a pass as a watchable clown but not Art the Clown isn't really going to be productive.<br /> <br /> To me the end result was predictably exactly what we're already seeing as some people are going to continue rationalizing not liking Beast as logical instead of preferential by shifting to a different axis of comparison. When a pod of orcas (and that would apply to Uratha if you strip away the fantasy filter when you remember what things like Hunter Aspects do) rather brutally harass prey, we accept that obligate carnivores have to hunt and eat, and accept that obligate predators are going to do things to optimize their survival strategies rather than &quot;hunt fair.&quot; Humans, who aren't obligate predators, performing many of the same hunting tactics we observe in orcas (even on prey animals, not even other humans) would be branded as cruel and inhumane precisely because that's a choice. But people are arguing that Beasts are bad because they're obligate predators instead of ones by choices; like hunters are. It doesn't really strike me as trying to defend a logical position, it strikes me as trying (poorly) to justify something that doesn't need justifications beyond preference.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Cauthon</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/1524397-hunters-pass-beasts-fail?p=1524410#post1524410\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Like said, playing a hunter whose proclivities turn your stomach is a deliberate choice, both in and out of character.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is an overly broad declaration. The Hunter groups are all designed with elements that are stomach turning even if a group of PCs don't engage with them. Network Zero might be an easy one to spin as unoffensive, but remember that they'll gladly hack anyone and everyone's personal data, stalk and violate privacy, and are driven by the desire to reveal the truth to the public; with little concerns for the consequences. Imagine having your whole life tossed into the shitter because a bunch of Internet sleuths with no accountability decide they need to document everything after you tried out a new bar with some friends and things got a bit hazy. You're all over YouTube and TikTok as proof of vampires existing. Selectively edited images, videos, sound clips, emails/texts/etc are all leaked to show that you're transforming into an undead monster. Would you be comforted by the fact that some of their friends just help ghosts find closure while knowing your life is being ruined and aren't doing anything about the still very alive you to get away from all their nutcase Internet friends?<br /> <br /> Even Hunter group is a moral compromise. Something you choose to engage with out of character by selecting/building them, and thus is a choice your character made to engage with in-character as well. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Beast, on the other hand, are <i>obligated</i> to be repugnant. </div> </div> </div>There's only one splat of them that's actually obligate to be repugnant. As we frequently point out around here, Beasts can easily choose lives that let them feed in fashions most people would fine morally neutral or even morally good; there are always people society wants to see suffer even if most of us don't like to see humanity that way.<br /> <br /> As well, as I noted above, that obligation is a mitigating circumstance for most moral and ethical considerations. Beasts die if they don't feed often enough. The impact of any Low Satiety and most Moderate Satiety, feedings are hardly things to condemn someone to starve to death over. Many of them are jobs people have. Many are things that are socially rewarded for doing.<br /> <br /> What example of a Moderate or Low Satiety feeding from the core book is so utterly morally reprehensible to you, that the fact that a live is on the line isn't enough to be worth letting happen?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> At best, you can go the &quot;family meal&quot; mechanic.., </div> </div> </div>Family Meal is more useful for having Satiety to spend freely during active play. No matter how much you get from this, a Beast still needs to sate their Hunger regularly on their own as well.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> ...or play the whole thing up as a personal tortured drama. </div> </div> </div>That is... the exact opposite of what Beast presents. One of the big negative reactions to Beast is that the characters aren't tortured about being monsters. They're angry that anyone thinks that monsters only exist to be part of someone else's story. The Begotten own their monstrosity and shape it towards their goals (Life and Legend in game terms).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> In Beast, -the core book at least, I've not read the rest- the narrative oozes an excuse plot to torture expies of people IRL the player doesn't like. </div> </div> </div>Are you sure you read the actual book, and not the SA review?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Vampire? Court politics. </div> </div> </div>How much of Requiem that overlaps with Beast - on top of how overly reductive all of this is - you left out is painfully obvious.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But Beast? Going off the core book alone -and that's all that most people are going to look at, IMO- there very little to the game beyond &quot;find target, torture them, possible kill them, repeat&quot;. The whole gameplay loop seems to center around hurting people, and -again, based on Core- treats anyone who fights against that as, at best, misguided. </div> </div> </div>Again, you sound like you read some of the more inane reviews online, not the actual book.<br /> <br /> Yes, Beast puts a big emphasis on feeding. But no more than plenty of other CofD games (Vampire and Werewolf in particular). The plot, as poorly presented in the core book because the core book isn't well edited, when one gets on with actual stories is about taking agency over one's own Legend and becoming something more than the final boss in a Hero's story via achieving an Inheritance. The core book might not explain it well, but it says this quite a lot.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":10},{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-23T07:02:51-07:00","id":1524422,"title":"","content":"You want to know the real reasons Hunter gets a pass and Beast doesn't? I can tell you two reasons. <br /> <br /> First, as I hinted above, Beast is different. Let's look at &quot;example of gaining satiety&quot;. In the example of play, the beast witnesses children being picked on, does nothing about it, then later breaks into the person's house, poisons the halloween candy, hides until the person eats the halloween candy, then torments them while they're sick. The only decent thing in that whole section is that the human being tormented is a &quot;frat boy&quot; (and thus an adult) even though his actions scream 13 year old, part of me wonders if the &quot;frat boy&quot; was edited in after that part was written and if it was written to originally be a younger teen. There's no life on the line in that example, there's no higher calling, there's no &quot;I'm doing this so I'm not a bit part in someone else's story&quot; it's just an abuser being abusive to someone they have power over. And this is the example from the book, it sets the expectation of play and given both it's location and tying into character creation it's something people will read early and will help shape their opinion of the splat. <br /> <br /> Now you can defend Beast here, it's one example, it doesn't have to be like that. Likewise you can throw other splats under the bus and scream how they can do horrible things. And the thing is, you wouldn't be wholly wrong, but you wouldn't be wholly right, either. I'm going to digress for a moment and look at Werewolf the Forsaken. <br /> <br /> The Uratha gain power from eating human flesh and that's not just fluff, it's mechanically supported, hell, there's even a lodge dedicated to eating human flesh in fact. That is as disgusting as anything you will find in the Beast book. So why is Beast so widely known as the abuser splat, but werewolf not the cannibal splat? Because, in werewolf, you're not supposed to eat human flesh, there's a rule against it, there's the taboo of it, but ultimately, it's always there as an option and as a horror element emphasizing that werewolves are not human and that they are, in fact, monsters. But in Beast being abusive isn't something you're supposed to avoid, it's not forbidden or taboo, it's just how you are supposed to act and feed as outlined by even the example in the book.<br /> <br /> So that's the first thing, but I told you I would give you two reasons. The other thing that contributes to Beast not getting a pass? Look around you at the posts in this forum. <br /> <br /> First though, go to the Hunters forum, look at a thread where a person is like &quot;I did a thing&quot; and look at the responses. &quot;Great idea.&quot; &quot;Definitely intriguing, will you be expanding upon it further&quot;. Hell, even in threads where it's not all &quot;wow&quot; the disagreement is polite and respectable. Now come back to the beast forum, what do you see? From accusations of not having read the book to telling people that any ideas counter to the book are both boring and mechanically horrible. People making posts in the two forums are not treated the same (which is especially mind boggling to me, because the people are the same people being helpful and respectful in one thread and then acting like this here).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27618-shadowcatx","name":"ShadowcatX","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27618_2.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"560"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-23T08:38:44-07:00","id":1524425,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/1524397-hunters-pass-beasts-fail?p=1524415#post1524415\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This is an overly broad declaration. The Hunter groups are all designed with elements that are stomach turning even if a group of PCs don't engage with them. Network Zero might be an easy one to spin as unoffensive, but remember that they'll gladly hack anyone and everyone's personal data, stalk and violate privacy, and are driven by the desire to reveal the truth to the public; with little concerns for the consequences. Imagine having your whole life tossed into the shitter because a bunch of Internet sleuths with no accountability decide they need to document everything after you tried out a new bar with some friends and things got a bit hazy. You're all over YouTube and TikTok as proof of vampires existing. Selectively edited images, videos, sound clips, emails/texts/etc are all leaked to show that you're transforming into an undead monster. Would you be comforted by the fact that some of their friends just help ghosts find closure while knowing your life is being ruined and aren't doing anything about the still very alive you to get away from all their nutcase Internet friends? <br /> Even Hunter group is a moral compromise. Something you choose to engage with out of character by selecting/building them, and thus is a choice your character made to engage with in-character as well.</div> </div> </div> </div> OK. And? I never said Compacts/Conspiracies were squeaky-clean paragons of morality, just that there are plenty of options to run with. The idea of &quot;Cancer Cells&quot; already shows that Hunters can be flawed, but doesn't make that the broad-strikes mentality of the book.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> There's only one splat of them that's actually obligate to be repugnant. As we frequently point out around here, Beasts can easily choose lives that let them feed in fashions most people would fine morally neutral or even morally good; there are always people society wants to see suffer even if most of us don't like to see humanity that way.<br /> <br /> As well, as I noted above, that obligation is a mitigating circumstance for most moral and ethical considerations. Beasts die if they don't feed often enough. The impact of any Low Satiety and most Moderate Satiety, feedings are hardly things to condemn someone to starve to death over. Many of them are jobs people have. Many are things that are socially rewarded for doing.<br /> <br /> What example of a Moderate or Low Satiety feeding from the core book is so utterly morally reprehensible to you, that the fact that a live is on the line isn't enough to be worth letting happen? </div> </div> </div> Anakim: &quot;The dreamer is left with no doubt that might makes right, and that the Giant has all the right in the world.&quot; Wow! Encouraging might-makes-right is <i>totally</i> a good idea!<br /> <br /> Eshamki: So our example characters are... a juvie Warden (not bad), someone murdering their extended family for their alledgedly stolen inheritance (understandable, if still repugnant), a dude who feeds his Horror by starving it to then murdering happy families (I'm like 90% sure that's not supposed to be how feeding works), and &quot;rich people bad, I'm totally not a hypocrite&quot;. Not a particularly compelling lineup.<br /> <br /> Makara: Instill a fear of death in people. Often by drowning people, if the examples are anything to go by. Charming.<br /> <br /> Namtaru: Aside from the Medusa bit, not bad.<br /> <br /> Ugallu: Chem-trail conspiracy bit aside, not bad.<br /> <br /> Tyrant: Intentionally be a petty asshole. Got it. Yes, that's a shallow reading, but of the examples given only the lawyer sounds somewhat reasonable, and <i>even then</i> you can read &quot;not because of the evidence&quot; as them intentionally getting innocent people locked up, just because they can.<br /> <br /> Collectors: Steal things from people. The Ugallu is kinda interesting, but the rest are uncompelling assholes.<br /> <br /> Predators: Hunt people and take trophies, only that last part is optional I guess? The examples are: werewolf-wannabe who murders; priest who hunts people for temptations he pushed them towards; kid who <i>traumatizes</i> would-be rescuers by pretending to drown then swimming away; weird razor-armed hugger who is somehow using that to make people care about other humans; kidnapper who intentionally causes familial strife. <i>Wow</i>, so compelling.<br /> <br /> Nemesis: Mostly OK, except for the couple who intentionally set up &quot;sunken treasure&quot; rumors so they could lure in victims whose only crime was &quot;adventure!&quot;. Oh, and the Ugallu are doing the thought police and entrapment thing.<br /> <br /> Ravager: Instill paranoia, salt the earth, weaponize cordyceps, murder for hire. 4/5 examples are not people I'd want to interact with.<br /> <br /> If the later books brought better clarity to the Horrors and Hungers, and if y'all on the forums have hammered out better interpretations, <i>great</i>. But I know very few people who are going to keep looking at a book line when the Core is rather unpleasant to get through. And that was the question - why do people give Hunter a pass? Because their core gives you latitude, and doesn't read like a bunch of assholes trying to justify their behavior. There are <i>some</i>, but they aren't the majority, especially with the meta-knowledge most CofD fans have about the other gamelines. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Family Meal is more useful for having Satiety to spend freely during active play. No matter how much you get from this, a Beast still needs to sate their Hunger regularly on their own as well. </div> </div> </div> So... <i>back</i> to mandatory abuse. Got it.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That is... the exact opposite of what Beast presents. One of the big negative reactions to Beast is that the characters aren't tortured about being monsters. They're angry that anyone thinks that monsters only exist to be part of someone else's story. The Begotten own their monstrosity and shape it towards their goals (Life and Legend in game terms). </div> </div> </div> ...yes? I may have phrased that poorly, I was implying that your options to approaching Satiety without the weird glee of the example characters was to either gather another gameline, or approach Feeding with a woe-is-me attitude. And I don't find the latter compelling.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Are you sure you read the actual book, and not the SA review? </div> </div> </div> Oh yes, the only way I could have a negative view of a gameline is by reading someone else's review. How droll.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> How much of Requiem that overlaps with Beast - on top of how overly reductive all of this is - you left out is painfully obvious. </div> </div> </div> You assume I like Requiem. I don't. It's a game I will <i>never</i> play. I can't say I even understand the popularity either line of Vampire has, beyond superpowers.<br /> <br /> But I can at least respect it for presenting the curse like a curse, and for having a gameplay loop I can point to and say &quot;ah, this is where we're expected to be interacting&quot;. I notice that of the ten examples of gameplay loop I presented, you only presented criticism for the one weakest, and no defense for Beast. Again, if the forums have managed to find one that works for them, great. But the Core book only seems to present &quot;hurt/abuse others, with the help of fellows, and maybe become the local boogeyman some day&quot;, which is nowhere near as compelling a premise to the average person as &quot;save the neighborhood from monstrous threats with your friends&quot;. So... yeah. That's my opinion on why Hunter gets a pass and Beast doesn't. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Yes, Beast puts a big emphasis on feeding. But no more than plenty of other CofD games (Vampire and Werewolf in particular). </div> </div> </div> I made my opinion on Vampire already. Werewolf plays the balancing act between being Flesh and Spirit, and while I will certainly concede that keeping that balance is a fraught endevor, there's still a gameplay loop outside of that which is clearly presented. Which brings me back once again to my overarching point: <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The plot, as poorly presented in the core book because the core book isn't well edited, when one gets on with actual stories is about taking agency over one's own Legend and becoming something more than the final boss in a Hero's story via achieving an Inheritance. The core book might not explain it well, but it says this quite a lot. </div> </div> </div> The presentation of Beast sucks. Once again, if the forums have found a way to make them work, great. If later books made them more palatable, <i>great</i>! But <i>Core does not</i>, and the majority of people aren't going to skin time and money into a game whose introduction isn't appealing. That's why, <i>in my opinion</i>, Hunter lacks the stigma of Beast.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\">Monkish Asexual.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1490057-cauthon-s-homebrew-hub#post1490057\" target=\"_blank\">My homebrew hub</a><br /> <br /> Mostly checked out, there's an uncomfortable amount of condescension in the old guard.</div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5},{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27258-cauthon","name":"Cauthon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27258_4.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1780"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-23T15:36:26-07:00","id":1524440,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ShadowcatX</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/1524397-hunters-pass-beasts-fail?p=1524422#post1524422\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">...part of me wonders if the &quot;frat boy&quot; was edited in after that part was written and if it was written to originally be a younger teen.</div> </div> </div> </div>It very much undermines your point when you do this. You're just admitting that a big part of the problem isn't the book, but people choosing to read it uncharitably. Brent, the victim of the Beast, is phrased as the owner of the house (there is candy on &quot;his coffee table&quot; which is not something you would say about a teen living with their parents), and he's alone enough for Mrs. Winters to poison his candy, hide, assault him further, use a Nightmare on him, threaten him, and turn the scene of the crime into a Chamber (something that requires far more brutality even if the Beast doesn't have to be the one that did it than normal feeding normally does) without any witnesses.<br /> <br /> How many 13 year olds do you know that have their own coffee tables, that still have candy from Halloween days/a week later, and can loudly vomit without anyone else living in the house coming to see what's going on? Additionally, since Mrs. Winter is the example character during character creation, it's established that Punishing children to feed is a big internal conflict for Mrs. Winter; not something she does lightly and Brent is described as an simple feeding for her. Punishing a child bully would be a much more significant character moment than the example presents the situation as.<br /> <br /> You're not criticizing the text, you're criticizing a fictional version of the text in your head.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> There's no life on the line in that example, there's no higher calling, there's no &quot;I'm doing this so I'm not a bit part in someone else's story&quot; it's just an abuser being abusive to someone they have power over. </div> </div> </div>The Beast's life is on the line if they don't feed in a manner that sate their hunger.<br /> <br /> The &quot;higher calling&quot; enough to be an Aspiration at least, is protecting the local children of the neighborhood.<br /> <br /> She is explicitly playing into her Maternal Life and Judgemental Legend with her actions, as a large point of what she does is to scare Brent into not making trouble (at least in Mrs. Winter's neighborhood) again. It is clearly not just abuse for the sake of exercising power. That doesn't make it a good way of handling things at all, but your analysis isn't really convincing.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And this is the example from the book, it sets the expectation of play and given both it's location and tying into character creation it's something people will read early and will help shape their opinion of the splat. </div> </div> </div>Again, this is hard to really see as the result of a blind read instead of a prejudged reading. &quot;The&quot; example comes have a literal page and a half of less detailed examples. It's over 100 pages into the book, with lots of other things impacting impressions. The repeated &quot;no neat boxes&quot; mantra of the text, this specific character being detailed as exploring a juxtaposition and a beloved grandmotherly figure that fights for the local public library, reads for the children, and other such things, with a monster lurking in the shadows, seeing all of the sins of the adults that have grown up and forgotten what &quot;good&quot; people are supposed to do, and is ready to judge and punish them for tarnishing her perfect neighborhood with their imperfections.<br /> <br /> The example in question is also abundantly clear that what happened is not something without consequences. Mrs. Winter did a lot of things that might come back to bite her in the ass in that example, and the book gives some ideas of what those might be. The book discusses in both the char-gen and example feeding that the player and ST are purposefully exploring a complex character that's going to have to deal with the higher order results of their immediate decisions. The game is at least trying to set expectations of making characters that are going to do things that will lead to dramatic conflicts. A lot of people complain about how RPG books don't do enough to encourage making characters more than two dimensional archetypes... and Beast goes deep into a character that is not that.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Now you can defend Beast here, it's one example, it doesn't have to be like that. Likewise you can throw other splats under the bus and scream how they can do horrible things. And the thing is, you wouldn't be wholly wrong, but you wouldn't be wholly right, either. </div> </div> </div>Your werewolf example doesn't really explain how we wouldn't be wholly right. Jumping straight to cannibalism in Forsaken is going past more basic examples like what Uratha are expected to do to ensure that The Herd Must Not Know. Cannibalism might be taboo, but murdering humans (unless you happen to have something that will just brainwash them instead, which isn't exactly a moral good to do) that accidentally see things they're not supposed to see is conversely required. There isn't a single splat in the CofD (including Hunters, as is the point of the thread and all) that aren't supposed to do things as bad if not far far worse than Beasts do at a base-line level.<br /> <br /> We're also just blowing right past that whether or not a book presents something as good or bad to do in-character, the books are still presenting things that are supposed to end up happening in the game. Uratha are supposed to be tempted into violating the Oath of the Moon and opting for some human snacks. It was actually considered a problem in Forsaken 1e that it was too taboo and too easy to avoid, so 2e made it much more likely for it to actually happen... because it's supposed to be part of the stories the game tells.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> First though, go to the Hunters forum,... </div> </div> </div>The Hunter forum haven't been plagued with deluges of posters starting threads about how Vigil needs to be fixed to stop being a game that glorifies fascist vigilante/extra-judicial violence, and people insisting that the game needs to be turned into &quot;eating the rich&quot; of various layers of metaphor/not so metaphor mixed with flat out bad mechanics to judge how &quot;rich&quot; and thus &quot;deserving&quot; capitalists are of this violence.<br /> <br /> This is one of the few non-private servers we can talk about Beast on. Most of the bigger forums have just banned it as a topic for being too contentious, or are cesspools of Internet flame-war culture that aren't actually worth engaging with. And people still feel the need to come in and just shit talk without anything constructive to say, while other people decide the Beast fans are the problem because we can't make Jesus and Buddha jealous of how saintly our patience and demeanors are in the face of it all, while the haters don't have any agency over their actions.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Cauthon</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/1524397-hunters-pass-beasts-fail?p=1524425#post1524425\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">OK. And?</div> </div> </div> </div>There's no such thing as a Hunter that is at a minimum complicit in stomach turning behavior. And worse than Beasts, they participate in this by choice. It is, in fact, a very prevalent part of Vigil that participating in monster hunting is bad for you on an emotional and moral level, an hunters are constantly flirting with the edge of turning into extremists that lost any moral high ground they might have started with. That's a very central theme of the game.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But I know very few people who are going to keep looking at a book line when the Core is rather unpleasant to get through. </div> </div> </div>You didn't answering my question: What example of a Moderate or Low Satiety feeding from the core book is so utterly morally reprehensible to you, that the fact that a live is on the line isn't enough to be worth letting happen? Summaries of the splats isn't an answer.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And that was the question - why do people give Hunter a pass? Because their core gives you latitude, and doesn't read like a bunch of assholes trying to justify their behavior. </div> </div> </div>Odd. Every Hunter group sounds like a bunch of assholes trying to justify their behavior to me. Beast offers just as much latitude in options to not play the worst of them.<br /> <br /> What makes Beast different in its writing, is that it's very blunt about the fact that the PCs are assholes trying to justify themselves. It's not a game about discovering that as you play. It's not a game where you figure that out over time. It smacks you upside the head with that, because the themes of the game focus on after accepting that the CofD is about playing assholes that try to justify themselves across the board.<br /> <br /> Which goes back to my first post in this thread: you're trying to turn a preference into something objective. You can just not like Beast's (and apparently Vampire's, but not a bunch of the other games?) more in-your-face approach to monstrosity. Because ultimately it's as simple as that.<br /> <br /> [quot]...yes? I may have phrased that poorly, I was implying that your options to approaching Satiety without the weird glee of the example characters was to either gather another gameline, or approach Feeding with a woe-is-me attitude. And I don't find the latter compelling.[/quote]<br /> <br /> The &quot;you have to be a gleeful abuser for the love of abuse&quot; vs &quot;woe-is-me&quot; setup is a false dichotomy explicitly out of line with the text of the book. You're setting up a strawman, and proceeding to beat it repugnantly.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Oh yes, the only way I could have a negative view of a gameline is by reading someone else's review. How droll. </div> </div> </div>When you make entire posts that seem to be mildly paraphrased regurgitations of the Something Awful review, to the point where it's rather noticeable, I'm going to call it out for you. Plagarize is rather droll.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You assume I like Requiem. </div> </div> </div>No, I assumed that your phrasing was purposefully negative in a fashion that belied your biases and wasn't actually a critical thought about the CofD. You've only reinforced that assumption.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> ...and no defense for Beast. </div> </div> </div>Well, if you didn't see my defense of Beast in there despite responding to it, it explains why your cherry pickings of the book sound so unoriginal.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But the Core book only seems to present &quot;hurt/abuse others, with the help of fellows, and maybe become the local boogeyman some day&quot;, which is nowhere near as compelling a premise to the average person as &quot;save the neighborhood from monstrous threats with your friends&quot;. So... yeah. That's my opinion on why Hunter gets a pass and Beast doesn't. </div> </div> </div>So, you're admitting that Hunter gets a pass purely on surface impressions, not anything actually in-depth about the games?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The presentation of Beast sucks. </div> </div> </div>Yes, we know. If that's your only point in all this, you have nothing worth saying.<br /> <br /> Arc addressed all of this part of the issues in the OP. The question is, &quot;how is a Hunter's Vigil meaningfully different from a Beast's Hunger in terms of the violence it commits?&quot;<br /> <br /> The initial impressions the books give isn't a sufficient answer, because it's not addressing the violence both groups actually do in play.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4},{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-23T16:14:19-07:00","id":1524443,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/1524397-hunters-pass-beasts-fail?p=1524440#post1524440\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> It very much undermines your point when you do this. You're just admitting that a big part of the problem isn't the book, but people choosing to read it uncharitably.</div> </div> </div> </div>Interesting that you felt the need to edit out all of my criticism of the actual paragraphs in order to accuse me of not actually criticizing the text itself, but that kind of thing goes to my second point. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Brent, the victim of the Beast, is phrased as the owner of the house (there is candy on &quot;his coffee table&quot; which is not something you would say about a teen living with their parents), and he's alone enough for Mrs. Winters to poison his candy, hide, assault him further, use a Nightmare on him, threaten him, and turn the scene of the crime into a Chamber (something that requires far more brutality even if the Beast doesn't have to be the one that did it than normal feeding normally does) without any witnesses.<br /> <br /> How many 13 year olds do you know that have their own coffee tables, that still have candy from Halloween days/a week later, and can loudly vomit without anyone else living in the house coming to see what's going on? </div> </div> </div>The same number of frat boys that I know that own their own house. The same number of frat boys that I know that go out and spend Halloween roughing up kids and stealing their candy rather than partying. How about you?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Additionally, since Mrs. Winter is the example character during character creation, it's established that Punishing children to feed is a big internal conflict for Mrs. Winter; not something she does lightly and Brent is described as an simple feeding for her. Punishing a child bully would be a much more significant character moment than the example presents the situation as. </div> </div> </div>\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200bDo you think having the example PC be a little conflicted about abusing children is a good argument towards beast not being an abuser splat?<br /> \u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You're not criticizing the text, you're criticizing a fictional version of the text in your head. </div> </div> </div>Yes, because you edited out my criticism of the actual text. Funny that. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The Beast's life is on the line if they don't feed in a manner that sate their hunger. </div> </div> </div>Nope, they specifically hunted just to have more points. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The &quot;higher calling&quot; enough to be an Aspiration at least, is protecting the local children of the neighborhood. </div> </div> </div>One would think if one wanted to protect the children one would intervene when the children were being bullied, no? <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> She is explicitly playing into her Maternal Life and Judgemental Legend with her actions, as a large point of what she does is to scare Brent into not making trouble (at least in Mrs. Winter's neighborhood) again. It is clearly not just abuse for the sake of exercising power. That doesn't make it a good way of handling things at all, but your analysis isn't really convincing. </div> </div> </div>Lol. Oh really? I feel like my analysis is what the roleplaying community at large came to and only a couple die hards on this forum feel otherwise. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Again, this is hard to really see as the result of a blind read instead of a prejudged reading. &quot;The&quot; example comes have a literal page and a half of less detailed examples. It's over 100 pages into the book, with lots of other things impacting impressions. The repeated &quot;no neat boxes&quot; mantra of the text, this specific character being detailed as exploring a juxtaposition and a beloved grandmotherly figure that fights for the local public library, reads for the children, and other such things, with a monster lurking in the shadows, seeing all of the sins of the adults that have grown up and forgotten what &quot;good&quot; people are supposed to do, and is ready to judge and punish them for tarnishing her perfect neighborhood with their imperfections. </div> </div> </div>So how much time does a guy who beats his wife have to spend at the library not to be an abuser? Oh right, it doesn't work like that. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The example in question is also abundantly clear that what happened is not something without consequences. Mrs. Winter did a lot of things that might come back to bite her in the ass in that example, and the book gives some ideas of what those might be. The book discusses in both the char-gen and example feeding that the player and ST are purposefully exploring a complex character that's going to have to deal with the higher order results of their immediate decisions. The game is at least trying to set expectations of making characters that are going to do things that will lead to dramatic conflicts. A lot of people complain about how RPG books don't do enough to encourage making characters more than two dimensional archetypes... and Beast goes deep into a character that is not that. </div> </div> </div>You're spending a lot of points trying to justify and excuse her being an abuser after claiming that my argument that she's an abuser didn't work... Doth thou protest too much? <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Your werewolf example doesn't really explain how we wouldn't be wholly right. Jumping straight to cannibalism in Forsaken is going past more basic examples like what Uratha are expected to do to ensure that The Herd Must Not Know. Cannibalism might be taboo, but murdering humans (unless you happen to have something that will just brainwash them instead, which isn't exactly a moral good to do) that accidentally see things they're not supposed to see is conversely required. </div> </div> </div>That's would be a fair point if killing people wasn't an expectation of every splat. But being a killer isn't the same as being an abuser, there are good reasons the Uratha have to kill people, there are no good reasons to commit abuse. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> There isn't a single splat in the CofD (including Hunters, as is the point of the thread and all) that aren't supposed to do things as bad if not far far worse than Beasts do at a base-line level. </div> </div> </div>Except no one else in CofD is expected to abuse people as Beasts are. Vampires come the closest as they are mostly required to feed from people at mid-level blood potency. But vampires are a known quantity, you can ask a random person how a vampire feeds and they will likely tell you vampires feed on blood. Beasts on the other hand, were created wholescale, the authors chose to make them feed on abusing people. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> We're also just blowing right past that whether or not a book presents something as good or bad to do in-character, the books are still presenting things that are supposed to end up happening in the game. Uratha are supposed to be tempted into violating the Oath of the Moon and opting for some human snacks. It was actually considered a problem in Forsaken 1e that it was too taboo and too easy to avoid, so 2e made it much more likely for it to actually happen... because it's supposed to be part of the stories the game tells. </div> </div> </div>I specifically pointed out that it has mechanics, actually. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The Hunter forum haven't been plagued with deluges of posters starting threads about how Vigil needs to be fixed to stop being a game that glorifies fascist vigilante/extra-judicial violence, and people insisting that the game needs to be turned into &quot;eating the rich&quot; of various layers of metaphor/not so metaphor mixed with flat out bad mechanics to judge how &quot;rich&quot; and thus &quot;deserving&quot; capitalists are of this violence. </div> </div> </div>So you don't deny people in this forum are abusive, including yourself, you just try and justify it. Like you justify the beasts being abusive. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> This is one of the few non-private servers we can talk about Beast on. Most of the bigger forums have just banned it as a topic for being too contentious, or are cesspools of Internet flame-war culture that aren't actually worth engaging with. </div> </div> </div>Gee, I wonder why so many people feel that way. It can't possibly be because of &quot;ineffective&quot; arguments like mine, right? <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And people still feel the need to come in and just shit talk without anything constructive to say, </div> </div> </div>Wow there's a lot to unpack here. You really dont see your own self righteousness do you? And the last thread I was looking at the guy didn't come in to shit talk, he came in to get input on his idea to help fix problems he saw with the game. It was the people like you who wanted to shit talk. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> while other people decide the Beast fans are the problem because we can't make Jesus and Buddha jealous of how saintly our patience and demeanors are in the face of it all, while the haters don't have any agency over their actions. </div> </div> </div>I'm not sure if you're delusional or if you just have been online too long, but &quot;don't abuse the newbies who come to ask questions about a roleplaying game&quot; is not exactly similar to &quot;make Jesus or Buddha jealous with our saintly patience&quot;. If you can't talk to someone without being a douche you shouldn't be talking. Ever think maybe your attitude is a big part of why Beast is banned as a topic of discussion elsewhere?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3},{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27618-shadowcatx","name":"ShadowcatX","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27618_2.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"560"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-23T17:11:28-07:00","id":1524444,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/1524397-hunters-pass-beasts-fail?p=1524440#post1524440\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> There's no such thing as a Hunter that is at a minimum complicit in stomach turning behavior. And worse than Beasts, they participate in this by choice. It is, in fact, a very prevalent part of Vigil that participating in monster hunting is bad for you on an emotional and moral level, an hunters are constantly flirting with the edge of turning into extremists that lost any moral high ground they might have started with. That's a very central theme of the game.</div> </div> </div> </div>So, just to play equivocation then. Anyone who works for Nestl\u00e9 is evil, down to the lowest wage-slave. Anyone who works telemarketing is evil. Anyone who works in a military, a police force, or for a government is evil. Because all parts of vaguely monolithic organizations are equally complicit in the actions of their fellows, much less loosely-affiliated people on the internet, right?<br /> <br /> Yeah, Hunting is bad for your health. So is just seeing something Supernatural. That's a thin argument at best.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You didn't answering my question: What example of a Moderate or Low Satiety feeding from the core book is so utterly morally reprehensible to you, that the fact that a live is on the line isn't enough to be worth letting happen? Summaries of the splats isn't an answer. </div> </div> </div>Quotes then?<br /> Collector: &quot;At moderate Satiety, not any example will do. This level requires some<br /> degree of refinement. A rare book collector can\u2019t just raid the local library if moderately Sated. But he might be sated by a local author\u2019s personal proof copy of her newest novel, with editing marks and notes in the margin.&quot; Sooooo... steal shit. Because the monster in your head tells you to.<br /> Anakim would be... ah yes, &quot;if they can't stand up to me, they didn't deserve to have it.&quot; So that rare book collector didn't deserve to have their book, because they couldn't fist-fight a Giant.<br /> Eshamki? &quot;Take their stuff and leave them paranoid of the dark&quot;. Yes, let's make every collector of rare objects into paranoid recluses, that's totally a good idea and not already a problem. Or steal family mementos, that's not petty.<br /> Makara? &quot;Rare historical objects hidden away from the world&quot; or &quot;murder people and hide their bodies in a cave off the shore&quot;<br /> Shall I go on?<br /> <br /> Tyrants: Moderate-level Satiety requires a reasonable but undeniable exertion of<br /> influence and shift of power dynamic to sate. A job promotion could apply, as could convincing an intern to work without pay, or starting and winning a bar fight against multiple (human) combatants. Blackmailing an influential person would count at this level.<br /> So we have Giants picking bar fights and intentionally provoking people. Blackmail, abuse of power. The job promotion is at least tame, though the fiction related to it sure as shit isn't.<br /> <br /> Prey: For high Satiety, a Beast must hunt challenging prey. This is prey that could kill<br /> her if she\u2019s not careful. It\u2019s also prey that has a distinct self-defense instinct, who can evade competently, and who can hide from anything short of a massive effort. Hunting supernatural beings is one way to reach this level of Satiety, as is hunting a very wealthy or famous human target.<br /> Moderate Satiety: The Beast must hunt things which pose some challenge, but aren\u2019t<br /> necessarily deadly threats outside of fringe circumstances. Most competent humans fall into this scale.<br /> On a 1-for-1 comparison, this is the closest you're going to get to a Hunter. On the other hand... &quot;That said, the Soul is usually happier if the Beast does kill.&quot; We have a word for Hunters that become like that, Slashers. They're an Antagonist, and one Beasts can <i>explicitly form Kinship with</i>, but not with Hunters.<br /> <br /> Ruin: For high Satiety, the destruction must be obvious and extensive, or the target must be widely accepted to be valuable. A Beast could destroy a large swath of property and then feast on the feelings of horror as the pictures go viral. She could kill a beloved celebrity, or smash a religious artifact<br /> Meduim, the item destroyed must be extremely important to at least a small group of people. The Beast might burn down a church (doing so with the congregation inside is actually not preferable, because then not as many people mourn the loss of the church), kill a local character that the neighborhood knows and loves, or smash all the windows of all the cars in a particular garage.<br /> So property damage. Because the voices in your head tell you to. I'll reiterate my points on the Collectors, because these are just Collectors who do more explict long-term harm.<br /> <br /> Punishment: For high Satiety, a Beast lingers over the punishment. Death traps, long sessions of torture, systematically hunting down and crippling the target\u2019s friends, or a slow death with a painful poison all count. The delicate balance here is making sure the target survives long enough to know why he\u2019s being punished.<br /> <i>Kinda weird how it emphasizes you only needing to keep them alive long enough to realize what's going on.</i> And this is HIGH Satiety.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Odd. Every Hunter group sounds like a bunch of assholes trying to justify their behavior to me. Beast offers just as much latitude in options to not play the worst of them.<br /> <br /> What makes Beast different in its writing, is that it's very blunt about the fact that the PCs are assholes trying to justify themselves. It's not a game about discovering that as you play. It's not a game where you figure that out over time. It smacks you upside the head with that, because the themes of the game focus on after accepting that the CofD is about playing assholes that try to justify themselves across the board. </div> </div> </div>You and I are getting <i>very</i> different reads of this text.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Which goes back to my first post in this thread: you're trying to turn a preference into something objective. You can just not like Beast's (and apparently Vampire's, but not a bunch of the other games?) more in-your-face approach to monstrosity. Because ultimately it's as simple as that. </div> </div> </div>Uuuuh... I really don't know how many more ways I can say &quot;this is my subjective opinion&quot; then I already have. So again. This is my subjective opinion.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The &quot;you have to be a gleeful abuser for the love of abuse&quot; vs &quot;woe-is-me&quot; setup is a false dichotomy explicitly out of line with the text of the book. You're setting up a strawman, and proceeding to beat it repugnantly. </div> </div> </div>You'll have to forgive me finding the glee in the Hunger quotes, Horror quotes, character examples, and fiction a bit hard to parse into anything but sadism. Since I need to state it again, apparently, my opinion, subjective not objective.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> When you make entire posts that seem to be mildly paraphrased regurgitations of the Something Awful review, to the point where it's rather noticeable, I'm going to call it out for you. Plagarize is rather droll. </div> </div> </div>Oh, take the sanctimony and shove it. I specifically ignored later Beast books in my assessment because I HAVE read the SA reviews on those. My opinion on Beast Core? All home grown.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Well, if you didn't see my defense of Beast in there despite responding to it, it explains why your cherry pickings of the book sound so unoriginal. </div> </div> </div>Mmmmmnope, I haven't seen you address what the gameplay loop of Beast is, no.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> So, you're admitting that Hunter gets a pass purely on surface impressions, not anything actually in-depth about the games? </div> </div> </div>Beast gives you superpowers, at the cost of being an asshat. Hunter gazes long into the Abyss, and fights to not become it. I find one more compelling than the other, but different strokes.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Arc addressed all of this part of the issues in the OP. The question is, &quot;how is a Hunter's Vigil meaningfully different from a Beast's Hunger in terms of the violence it commits?&quot;<br /> <br /> The initial impressions the books give isn't a sufficient answer, because it's not addressing the violence both groups actually do in play. </div> </div> </div>One fights monsters that (usually, big disclaimer) actively hurt others, but has the potential to become a monster themself. The other <i>must</i> actively hurt others to survive, and at best becomes a worse monster through Inheritance.<br /> <br /> And if the point is &quot;why do people like one but not the other&quot;? I have no way of knowing how you play your game, all I have is the impressions the book gives. If you have a legit Actual Play for me to read, I'll take it. But otherwise the book is <i>all anyone has to go on unless they've played it, and that impression doesnt draw a lot of players.</i><br /> <br /> But, my mistake. I legitimately didn\u2019t realize this question involving multiple Splats was in the Beast channel, not the general channel. 'S what I get for just having a mass CofD sub. I'll leave you to your echo chamber.<br /> <br /> Edit -<br /> OK, because this was bothering me: <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The initial impressions the books give isn't a sufficient answer, because it's not addressing the violence both groups actually do in play. </div> </div> </div> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Odd. Every Hunter group sounds like a bunch of assholes trying to justify their behavior to me. Beast offers just as much latitude in options to not play the worst of them. </div> </div> </div> Which is it, book presentation or how people play? Because you can't have it both ways. And even then, Hunter has mechanics for building your own Compacts and Conspiracies if the written ones aren't to your taste. Being a violent extremist vigilante is still a <i>choice</i>, and <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/1504062-so-i-m-currently-reading-the-2nd-edition?p=1514914#post1514914\">I've made my case in the past that even a Charon team doesn't have to be mustachioed villains.</a> Beast <i>doesn't have that.</i> <br /> <br /> Please, prove me wrong and point to the page number(s) where their book outlines how to be a Beast without abusing others. I've not even touched the fact that Heroes are more likely to be drawn to Beasts who fight against their urge to Feed. But if those workarounds and justifications only exists in forum posts, well... I hate to break it to you but <i>no one cares about homebrew</i>. Publish it on the Storyteller's Vault and see if it gets any traction, I will be legitimately happy for you if it does. But at the end of the day Hunter can be used out of the box without the ick of being a horribly presented and executed allegory. Beast can't. <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\">Monkish Asexual.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1490057-cauthon-s-homebrew-hub#post1490057\" target=\"_blank\">My homebrew hub</a><br /> <br /> Mostly checked out, there's an uncomfortable amount of condescension in the old guard.</div>","edited":{"userid":27258,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27258-cauthon","user":"Cauthon","date":". 10-25-2023, 02:32 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2},{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27258-cauthon","name":"Cauthon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27258_4.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1780"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-23T17:14:32-07:00","id":1524445,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>21C Hermit</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/1524397-hunters-pass-beasts-fail?p=1524411#post1524411\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">'Meaningful' difference. I guess the differences usually brushed away are meaningful after all? Beauty is skin deep, but the skin is fricking big and important. I mean, people want to be vindicated. Hunters in Hunter often are. Beasts in Beast rarely are.<br /> <br /> Actually, on surface it feels the opposite - Hunter doesn't particularly hide that you're playing violent vigilantes wrapped up in potential conspiracy theory material, while Beast does its best to assure that you are fighting for your own story, etc. I blame unintended reverse psychology at work here.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Expanding on what I said, and returning to the Hunter-Beast comparison... I think another key difference is the presence/absence of foils.<br /> <br /> Almost all 'protagonist' splats have eviler-than-thou counterparts or 'fail states' that have the same starting point as them (as in, without being a completely different sort of creature). They serve as images of what you could be if you unleash the worst inside you, and what twisted form you can fall into if you can't hold it together.<br /> <br /> <br /> Hunters have other Compacts or Conspiracies (usually the latter) as their eviler-than-thou counterparts. You want to emphasize the atrocities of unchecked government power? This time, the Task Force Valkyrie are foils. You want to emphasize the dangers of communities becoming xenophobic echo chambers in real life? That's tough to do, but the Union could be twisted to that end.<br /> <br /> We also have Slashers, who are in-universe questionnaires of Maybe Not So Different handed out to all hunters.<br /> <br /> <br /> Beast... seems to lack such foils. Which feeds into the alleged suspicions that Beast tries a bit too hard for its own good in justifying &amp; making excuses for its protagonist splat's monstrosities.<br /> <br /> Yes, other Beasts are presented as eviler-than-thou foils, just like other vampires and demons often are in their respected games, but they aren't exempt from the air of Just Trying To Be Myself that Beast core as-is grants to all Beasts. Meanwhile, a vampire is always a fricking vampire.<br /> <br /> <br /> On my last point; I'll have to reread Beast core and Conquering Heroes to see if that really holds. (Can't guarantee that I will; I've got things to do) But speaking as someone who <i>accepts</i> Beast as a 'proper' CofD line, the meta-knowledge that even someone like I am left with such an impression bodes ill...","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>MtAw Homebrew:<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1051456-even-more-legacies-updated-to-2e#post1051456\" target=\"_blank\">Even more Legacies, updated to 2E</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1443819-new-2e-legacies-expanded\" target=\"_blank\">New 2E Legacies, expanded</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12619-21c-hermit","name":"21C Hermit","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12619_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3489"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-23T18:11:27-07:00","id":1524448,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ShadowcatX</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/1524397-hunters-pass-beasts-fail?p=1524443#post1524443\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That's would be a fair point if killing people wasn't an expectation of every splat. But being a killer isn't the same as being an abuser, there are good reasons the Uratha have to kill people, there are no good reasons to commit abuse.</div> </div> </div> </div>There is not great exemplar of how bad faith all of this is.<br /> <br /> &quot;Murdering people is fine, but non-lethal assaults are never acceptable,&quot; is one hell of a take.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-23T18:32:32-07:00","id":1524452,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/1524397-hunters-pass-beasts-fail?p=1524448#post1524448\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-sc