name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-23T20:14:56-07:00","id":1524465,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar99_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"99\" data-vbnamecard=\"99\">ArcaneArts</a> we made as much of a point as we're going to. Time to sit back, grab a glass, and wait for the mods.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":2},{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-23T20:17:44-07:00","id":1524466,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/1524397-hunters-pass-beasts-fail?p=1524465#post1524465\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar99_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"99\" data-vbnamecard=\"99\">ArcaneArts</a> we made as much of a point as we're going to. Time to sit back, grab a glass, and wait for the mods.</div> </div> </div> </div>I was already setting up for that, since I've got work tomorrow, and I've got me my whiskey, gin, and teeth for the wind-down.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-23T20:21:33-07:00","id":1524467,"title":"","content":"The gist of it, I think, is in the starting points.<br /> <br /> So way back in 2017 when the previews for Hunter 2e were coming out there was a discussion about the Code where I made a point that I can definitely word better after having read through that game's First Edition in full: While it would be a lie to say that Hunter is not a game for which The True Monster Is Man is a recurring sub-theme, the Vigil's generally maladaptive capacity to respond to that gradual revelation is a bit less front-loaded.<br /> <br /> Yes, 1e included in its Antagonist section extremely clear messy-enough-to-include-other-stuff categories that directly corresponded to the protagonists of the preceding five gamelines, had the order of its main supplements focused on monster types come out with basically the two most human monster-types first, and made no secret of the fact that a prominent facet of expected information-gathering from monsters is likely to involve torture, to say nothing of the multiple neutral/exploitative/power-preservation-oriented lenses on the Vigil introduced throughout the line, but the foundational archetypes of splat are primed as reactively/protectively motivated in origin despite the protagonists' ongoing decision to crack their bones against the wall of magic and power and conspiracy until they're numb to the concrete suffering of individuals in service to a drive that is frequently violent and jealous and paranoid and reactionary \u2014 people are a lot more prepared to play the hotheaded altruists whose family got eaten by werewolves from the jump than to deal with the splat's survivorship bias being coldly paranoid and willing to let people get hurt to draw the monsters further out into the open.<br /> <br /> Whatever your Hunger and typical level of fullness, the Devouring explicitly involves accepting that you and the monster are the same being, taking and intimidating because that's a key part of what you are, and the rest of your character arc from that starting point involves figuring out what you want to do with that and what else about you matters. &quot;Welcome to Hunter, you're not the Good Guys&quot; is an eventual realization for the casual reader, whereas &quot;Just because you are Bad Guy doesn't mean you are bad guy&quot; is a thing the Beast corebook makes somewhat difficult to get to thanks to the intensity of the language it uses to describe the generalities of Begotten activities \u2014 reading the books, Beasts in general are operating at the universal standard when placed in context with their fellow monsters (to say nothing of the fact that Family fellow-feeling doesn't undo the very solid reason to want an Apex who isn't a Beast in your town), but when words like &quot;legendary monster&quot; and &quot;incarnate nightmare&quot; are being used at all levels of the scale it tends to obfuscate the fact that the Begotten are powered by Significant Actions at a lower level than Godzilla Activities.<br /> <br /> To be extremely reductive, it's part of why Changeling keeps getting people asking for Good Guy True Fae \u2014 Hunter can more easily check the escapist power fantasy boxes of having a traumatic origin story and kicking monster ass when the core drive behind doing so isn't set up from the jump as e.g. &quot;you want power so you don't have to suffer the soul-shaking fear of not being in control of your life,&quot; whereas &quot;you took power because you recognized that you could be like the thing that scared you at a soul-deep level&quot; as a starting point for a more contemplative story arc about why something is scary and how that power can be used is a little less universally beloved as a narrative. People like their Heroes, and getting people to question a long-established narrative can be difficult.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":111,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","user":"Satchel","date":". 10-23-2023, 11:24 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":10},{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-24T20:30:09-07:00","id":1524497,"title":"","content":"Honestly I think it all comes down to some of the issues in how it was presented. Beast was my introduction to the Chronicles of Darkness, so when I read it I just took for granted that, for instance, beast was this kinda existentialist game that expected players to decide for themselves where they drew the line between acceptable feeding behavior and going off the deep end and the somewhat discordant authorial voice was meant to highlight that (the fact that my only point of reference at that point was D&amp;D may have played a role). Then I looked into it a bit more and started reading that Beast was actually about abusers abusing and that I was a morally bad person for liking it. That didn't jibe with vibes I'd gotten, but it didn't feel like there was much in the corebook I could point to show it <i>wasn't </i>the case. In contrast, if my introduction to the Chronicles was the 2e hunter book I'd probably have a much easier time disputing the assertion that the Hunter 2e book was apologia for, say, Kyle Rittenhouse and the militia movement. Basically even if the violence of Beast isn't inherently worse than than violence of Hunter, Hunter still better at explaining itself and therefore less likely to get this sort of criticism.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27692-alexanderalaska","name":"alexanderalaska","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-25T14:35:02-07:00","id":1524507,"title":"","content":"Wow, didn't know this topic was still... a topic. Huh.<br /> <br /> What now proceeds are my hopefully somewhat coherent ramblings on topics I've seen come up while skimming this thread. Please be patient with me.<br /> <br /> First of all: I don't really get the problem, I have to admit.<br /> The concept of &quot;person with monstrous urges tries to arrange themselves with the situation&quot; is not exactly new. Vampires try not to suck people dry, uratha try not to snack on people, Beasts try not to be a source of harm for people close to them by not Feeding, but sometimes, accidents happen, or the situation calls for it, or your character had a <i>really </i>bad day and the dice weren't nice...so what are you gonna do? Survive. Deal with the outfall of whatever foolhardy or stupid decision was made. Learn from it. Avoid in the future. Rinse and repeat.<br /> <br /> I was under the impression that this is <i>precisely </i>the intent of the games: to create dramatical situations, whether they be things like &quot;Vampire is stuck between a rock and a hard place and killing this human he likes would make all the problems go away&quot;,&quot;Mage should better not touch this button to satisfy his curiousity&quot;, &quot;Promethean misunderstands a core concept of humanity, resulting in tragedy&quot; or &quot;Changeling paranoia leads to bad times and broken relationships&quot; - things <i>need </i>to go sideways, because conflict feeds the story, and these are horror stories at their core. Something should make you horrified - shocked, repulsed.<br /> Playing a character that might have to e.g. abuse the trust other people have in him as their psychotherapist to manipulate them into doing very bad things for themselves (as Enablers are wont to do)? Absolutely horrifying idea to me. I also relished playing this character and his seeking to come to terms with what is the ultimate betrayal in his point of view (sadly game ended too soon, so I don't know where we'd have ended up with him). Do I get that not everyone would want to play this? Absolutely.<br /> However: I don't see why one &quot;I need to do terrible thing x and try to find a way to live with it&quot; should be different from the rest.<br /> I get it is more niche than the others, but still, why all the hate?<br /> Not going to go into the already made statements regarding complex characters making partly bad decisions (with possibly good intentions) that will come back to bite them in the ass being something some of us like to explore in games. Or personal horror. Or playing monsters rather than supowerpowered friendly neighbourhood vampires (<b>not </b>saying that is a wrong way to play, nor that you couldn't play Beast that way.)\u200b<br /> <br /> Secondly, I also feel like people are purposely trying to make splats like Hunter look good in comparison to Beast through their choices of character.<br /> Batman could very easily be a Beast template. Nemesis Eshmaki, teaching the criminal underworld to &quot;fear the Batman&quot;. Would you call him an abuser?<br /> What about a cop who leaves the confines of his profession to go after criminals that slip through the system, like Martin Riggs and Roger Murtaugh from Lethal Weapon? Cops working outside the law, outright breaking international law to quite literally punish 'evildoers'? Abusers? I'll admit they mostly just kill their enemies, though.<br /> (In fact, jobs like police and prison wardens could be perfect for Beasts to provice opportunities to Feed on low Satiety.)<br /> Dexter could easily be a Beast. Is Dexter an abuser? No, pretty sure he's a serial killer. But is being a serial killer really that much better?<br /> Let's take Lupin, the master thief. Someone stealing precious belongings to cause emotional anguish? Maybe even going full anime and sending a fancy card to announce the upcoming theft? Make them worry first, grieve later. Quite the Collector.<br /> Heck, Robin Hood could be a Tyrant trying to offset his bad conscience due to all the people he kills during the robberies by giving it to the poor, thereby feeding into his own self-image of grandiouse generosity or justice.<br /> <br /> Re: Beasts are inherently more 'evil' than other splats<br /> Thirdly, I don't think Beasts are in any way shape or form <i>inherently or objectively </i>worse than other splats.<br /> Some, sure. But bad Beasts are more akin to Bale Hounds than Forsaken. Almost every splat has a faction embracing a monstrous darkness and as a result is considered something of a cause for an enemy-mine truce-fire situation. VII, Scelesti, Bale Hounds, Loyalists etc. Beasts have monsters like these too, but since you don't have as neat of a social axis, they are harder to identify, and don't quite fit the same mold.<br /> In general, though, every splat comes into situations where they could not harm the general populace, but still do so, to fulfill a personal need: curiousity, lust for power, pure survival instinct.<br /> Are Beasts egoistic for Feeding? I'd say not more than anyone else: if not doing something could kill you, you'd find a way to do it, even if you dislike doing it. Especially if not doing it comes with its own host of problems. Are Beasts encouraged to act badly? Yes. But that tension of &quot;where is the character drawing the line and why&quot; makes for interesting stories. Not having a Masquerade that makes certain demands of the character nor an &quot;you lose control and the Beast/Kuruth makes you do bad stuff&quot; mechanic makes acting or not acting on the Hunger more meaningful, and places more responsibility on the character (Not shitting on the Kuruth/Frenzy mechanics here, they are wonderful in their own gameline and offer different story opportunities).<br /> And I like that.<br /> <br /> Furthermore, I have often wondered why so many people fixate on this &quot;Beasts are abusers, and thus the worst!&quot; concept, and actually asked myself whether it is due to a lack of dissociation on part of some readers; lethal violence and murderous intent are, after all, not something most of us typically experience, and as such, depictions of it are cathartic or, at worst, offputting.<br /> Non-lethal means of violence, however, are within the realm of experience to some degree, and thus has a much higher chance to hit close to home, especially when there is not a huge veil like &quot;for the sake of the Masquerade&quot; on it, leaving it quite clear that this action is always a choice of the character. Maybe that is why so many dislike it? I don't know.<br /> Are some Beasts abusers? Sure. Like every Kindred with Ghouls, or a Herd, or abusing their power to, well, abuse humans and lower-ranked Kindred. Doesn't mean they all are. Some are simply more lucky than others, and exploring that can be a worthwhile experience.<br /> <br /> Re: &quot;Different proclivities&quot;<br /> Finding a way deal productively with deeply seated destructive needs and grasping for agency in one's own life is a story I find interesting, moreso than vampire politics, for example.<br /> I understand this is not the case for all people - to each their own, live and let live. Where does this constant need to put down Beast come from? If one dislikes something, one only needs to simply stay away - no more contact with the contentious topics or problems or sheer insanity of the others means more happiness. Why willingly drive into this again and again and again?<br /> <br /> <br /> Not saying Beast is perfect (nobody's <i>ever </i>said this on this forum, afaik), quite the opposite. The sharpest criticism comes from those among us who adore it the most, I'd say. &quot;A Beautiful Mess&quot;, was it? But if we see worth in it, want to elevate it to what we believe it could've always been, why not just wait and see? Why this constant needling? Not rhetorical, genuinely trying to understand here.\u200b<br /> <br /> /rant<br /> <br /> Thank you for your patience.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Satchel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/1524397-hunters-pass-beasts-fail?p=1524467#post1524467\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">&quot;Welcome to Hunter, you're not the Good Guys&quot; is an eventual realization for the casual reader, whereas &quot;Just because you are Bad Guy doesn't mean you are bad guy&quot; is a thing the Beast corebook makes somewhat difficult to get to thanks to the intensity of the language it uses to describe the generalities of Begotten activities.</div> </div> </div> </div>OMG. This made me smirk, then think. I cannot believe you just summed up this whole rant and more with a friggin Wreck it Ralph quote. Moments like this are why I keep coming back to these boards.<br /> \u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":8542,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8542-wormwood","user":"Wormwood","date":". 10-25-2023, 05:42 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":7}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8542-wormwood","name":"Wormwood","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8542_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1198"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-26T12:04:29-07:00","id":1524536,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TyrannicalRabbit</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">I won't sugarcoat it. I don't like Beast, it feels palatably gross in a way that the other games don't. And not gross in the way say an old school horror movie can be delightfully schlocky. I've both seen and experienced gaslighting about it. I've yet to see something really fruitful gained from comparing it to other game lines favourably or unfavourably.<br /> <br /> At the end of the day though what this means is I don't run Beast, and Beast flat out doesn't exist in my version of the CofD universe. I don't think ill of people who do like it or run it or play it and I'd say there are ideas in it that would form the bedrock of a great second edition.<br /> <br /> I wish people good gaming but no thank you.</div> </div> </div> </div>That seems an entirely understandable position to have and healthy approach on the whole thing imo. Don't like it? Leave it. There's enough out there.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8542-wormwood","name":"Wormwood","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8542_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1198"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-26T14:30:43-07:00","id":1524550,"title":"","content":"I think it's worth noting that Arc is working on (in a fan capacity) a new edition of Beast (and has posted plenty of things about that here). While it's relatively simple to address BtP's incredibly poor editing on technical issues (aka do things that the lead developer should have done, but didn't bother doing), it doesn't get to fixing some of the other issues with the game. Arc's spent a lot of time on reevaluating Legend and Heroes and how to make both more effective parts of the game.<br /> <br /> But that visceral negative reaction is something else to address. The effort could just not try and count on existing fans alone, but if there are people that would give BtP the chance with a new edition if something was done about the negative gut reaction people have had... it's worth trying to figure out if that's possible. Past discussions about this have frequently turned to what I labelled the &quot;fantasy filter.&quot; While a generalization, a lot of people have expressed it in various ways. It's not an unreasonable response by any means, even if it doesn't really offer up much in the way of how to adjust Beast for a 2e that's more palatable to a wider audience.<br /> <br /> The point of comparing Beast to Hunter came in because, as noted in the OP, hunters have a vastly reduced fantasy filter. A cell or compact taking on a slasher doesn't inherently involve any fantasy elements, but still doesn't get the same &quot;gross&quot; response that Beast gets. Hence the question of the thread. It's entirely possible nobody has an answer that will help reframe Beast 2e in a better light, but if there is insight out there, the only way to find it is to ask.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-26T18:07:39-07:00","id":1524559,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TyrannicalRabbit</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">I won't sugarcoat it. I don't like Beast, it feels palatably gross in a way that the other games don't. And not gross in the way say an old school horror movie can be delightfully schlocky. I've both seen and experienced gaslighting about it. I've yet to see something really fruitful gained from comparing it to other game lines favourably or unfavourably.<br /> <br /> At the end of the day though what this means is I don't run Beast, and Beast flat out doesn't exist in my version of the CofD universe. I don't think ill of people who do like it or run it or play it and I'd say there are ideas in it that would form the bedrock of a great second edition.<br /> <br /> I wish people good gaming but no thank you.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's great Rabbit, but this has nothing to do with the question of how people square Hunter's Violence with Beast's violence. If I wanted <i>just</i> an expression of disdain for the gameline, I have no shortage of threads and posts to visit.<br /> <br /> I need perspective. It doesn't need to be positive perspective-I expected a lot of negative takes, and there's a reason only one particular bit of it got commented on so far-but I need to get into people's heads on this subject.<br /> <br /> I've gotten this take from you you before, and it was valuable the first time, but it's not useful here with what I'm asking. If you do have a particular insight on how you differentiate between Hunter's violence and Beast's violence, I'd be glad to hear it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2},{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-26T19:47:08-07:00","id":1524565,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Cauthon</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/1524397-hunters-pass-beasts-fail?p=1524410#post1524410\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You can play a Hunter whose only interactions with the supernatural are inquisitive and/or warding - Network Zero dudes helping ghosts move on, Wildlife Solutions response teams isolating and rehabilitating cryptids and chimera, even TF:V teams living out episodes of Eureka/Warehouse 13. Like said, playing a hunter whose proclivities turn your stomach is a deliberate choice, both in and out of character.<br /> <br /> Beast, on the other hand, are <i>obligated</i> to be repugnant. At best, you can go the &quot;family meal&quot; mechanic, or play the whole thing up as a personal tortured drama. But you know what? That &quot;woe is me, I am so monstrous and must harm others to survive!&quot; tripe is barely tolerable in Vampire. In Beast, -the core book at least, I've not read the rest- the narrative oozes an excuse plot to torture expies of people IRL the player doesn't like.<br /> <br /> And actually, that's the thing that makes me unable to treat Beast like I would any other splat, including Hunter. The rest all have a... theme? Plot?<br /> You go into a Mage game, you generally expect to be solving mysteries.<br /> Werewolf? Keep the local Shadow in order, be it from Hosts, Pure, Magath, Idagam, etc.<br /> Sin-Eater? Help ghosts, explore the Underworld.<br /> Vampire? Court politics.<br /> Changeling? Ditto, with a side of avoiding the Fae and possibly reclaiming your old life.<br /> Mummy? On a mission from Gods.<br /> Demon? Flee the God-Machine, make Cover, Interlocks, build Hell,<br /> Promethean? Become human.<br /> Deviant? Fight/flee the Web, master your scars.<br /> Hunter? Combat that which goes bump in the night.<br /> But Beast? Going off the core book alone -and that's all that most people are going to look at, IMO- there very little to the game beyond &quot;find target, torture them, possible kill them, repeat&quot;. The whole gameplay loop seems to center around hurting people, and -again, based on Core- treats anyone who fights against that as, at best, misguided.</div> </div> </div> </div>The impression I got is that the book gives you that you should try to be the apex or use your skills to rise within an organization while you deal with your own needs and other supernaturals, but I imagine they fit better being part of other groups.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27832-psicose","name":"Psicose","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27832_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"892"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-26T19:49:49-07:00","id":1524566,"title":"","content":"Another point.<br /> <br /> CofD games seem to have a Light part and a Heavy part. The Light part is the \u2018fun\u2019 part where we get to be monsters trucking with other monsters, playing out varying degrees of dark fantasies and gallows humour. The Heavy part is the part where we look at the ramifications of monstrosity interacting with humanity, and hopefully find catharsis.<br /> <br /> The Heavy part is what makes the CofD games distinct from other games, but the Light part is what makes us try them in the first place. More importantly, we can appreciate the games solely from the Light part, without diving deeper in to the Heavy. Indeed, not all people bother to do so. (And this is <b>not</b> some sort of intellectual laziness, or some sign of immaturity, or some sort of social injustice. It\u2019s just how people are.)<br /> <br /> Hunter follows the above formula. Get in the game for grittier-than-usual urban fantasy-ish monster hunting in our own world. The No Fantasy Filter On Violence part? The violence here is actually on the Heavy part. (\u201cReflection on vigilante violence and conspiracy<br /> theories\u201d? That\u2019s Heavy as you can get be!) Hence the lack of \u2018filters\u2019 matters much less, because the people seeing it are already at the point of having fine-tuned their gamer minds to look at it.<br /> <br /> Now Beast. Beast\u2019s Light part is being a nightmare monster. Who\u2026 hurts and shocks people. Okay\u2026 that\u2019s quite a hurdle to just appreciating the game on its own merit. The \u2018filter\u2019-less part slams people straight from the Light part. This requires some explanation from the Heavy part to make sense and be appreciated. Except that the Heavy part is, as said above, not the place most people go to when the Light part already failed to hook them.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Now, the existence of Beast fans and devotees obviously means that the \u2018formula\u2019 isn\u2019t an infallible axiom or something. Never intended to present one! But I believe it\u2019s a model that explains a lot.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>MtAw Homebrew:<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1051456-even-more-legacies-updated-to-2e#post1051456\" target=\"_blank\">Even more Legacies, updated to 2E</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1443819-new-2e-legacies-expanded\" target=\"_blank\">New 2E Legacies, expanded</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12619-21c-hermit","name":"21C Hermit","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12619_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3489"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-26T21:02:59-07:00","id":1524570,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Psicose</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/1524397-hunters-pass-beasts-fail?p=1524565#post1524565\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The impression I got is that the book gives you that you should try to be the apex or use your skills to rise within an organization while you deal with your own needs and other supernaturals, but I imagine they fit better being part of other groups.</div> </div> </div> </div>Broadly speaking, yeah, the main things one aims for as a Beast before they've burned all their bridges involve being gregarious among the local monsters and ingratiating them to you by facilitating their feeding and continued activities. The useful thing about the combination of Family Dinner and the Hungers is that they allow the Begotten to be notable lieutenants and fixers as well as End Bosses \u2014 the corebook's material focused more on characters being prominently infamous and upsetting the apple cart, which makes it a little harder to get a grasp on the day-to-day operations of a hive's Beasts running stealth, much like Mummy does not actually work under the assumption that its titular characters will be throwing around apocalyptic powers at every arising because cover-ups take time for their cults to orchestrate.<br /> <br /> Even without bringing in Primordial Cults from the Player's Guide, a version of Beast that had some iteration of the organization mechanics to work with would probably look notably different if only because it would have a counterbalance in the full breadth of starting Satiety ratings a character can have \u2014 whether you're pulling the strings or running interference, lots of supernatural societies (especially ad-hoc ones) need enforcers, acquisitors, investigators, and otherwise persuasive individuals who can tie a bunch of disparate weirdoes together.<br /> <br /> As you get stronger and hungrier it becomes harder not to burn bridges without sectioning the other end off from the mainland, but a better-managed go at the material would theoretically strike the balance between &quot;despite everything, you and these monsters understand each other&quot; and &quot;make no mistake, though, they <i>are</i> terrible people, because it's hard not to be in these circumstances and one takes their joys where one can.&quot; Making the right friends and stewarding one's family ties means making moves to control the streets around a haunted alley, preparing bribes and/or blackmail to keep witnesses from ratting out your sewer-gator kin to Animal Control, riding along on a vampire's club night, and managing your relationships well enough that maybe-just-maybe those three things can get along and find common ground with the animal magnetism of your greedy toothy lindwurm-Horror what's currently doing a brisk trade as a local mascot/memetic grafitto. Eventually \u2014 for some sooner than others \u2014 a Beast will fail to keep up in the Red Queen's Race of making sure the lights stay off (or simply get sick of doing so) and hurt the people closest to them enough that they can't run it back, at which point they're back on the uglier side of monstrous upkeep like any other monster who hasn't got a secret society to help support them.<br /> <br /> The tricky thing about the game where you play the human face of a storied cryptid is that leaning too hard into what the storied cryptid does is as uncomfortable as leaning into the dark side of any other splat, and that's usually reserved for climactic points in the story for a reason.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-27T09:11:16-07:00","id":1524590,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ArcaneArts</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/1524397-hunters-pass-beasts-fail#post1524397\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Square the circle-how is a Hunter's Vigil meaningfully different from a Beast's Hunger in terms of the violence it commits?</div> </div> </div> </div>The meaningful difference from my understanding of both games about the violence Hunters commit versus the violence Beasts commit is that there IS NO meaningful difference. However, the presentation of Hunter is that it eases you into the violence by letting you believe that there are acceptable targets for the violence. Beast is more open with the idea that violence is what matters and isn't shy about making it clear that &quot;acceptable targets&quot; is an excuse Beasts can use to justify their violence.<br /> <br /> It calls it out hard by highlighting an entire Hunger around the concept with the Hunger for Punishment.<br /> <br /> Honestly, to me, it really highlights the narrative force of the Hero vs the Monster, where violence on the Hero's part is counted as justifiable (Hunters) and violence on the Monster's part is counted as repugnant (Beasts).<br /> <br /> Thinking on it... I guess a meaningful difference between the two is that some people really want there to be a meaningful difference. And this is how atrocities are lauded.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/851-paradim","name":"Paradim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar851_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"695"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-29T12:23:10-07:00","id":1524700,"title":"","content":"Okay, I admittedly haven't really read what's been posted on this thread in-depth beyond what ArcaneArts has written for herself in the OP, so I'mma just post my personal response to the fundamental question she proposed;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ArcaneArts</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/1524397-hunters-pass-beasts-fail#post1524397\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Square the circle-how is a Hunter's Vigil meaningfully different from a Beast's Hunger in terms of the violence it commits?</div> </div> </div> </div>Based on what little I know of Hunter: the Vigil; not really if you're speaking about the violence by itself. As for general justifications, based on what I've gathered, Beasts who <i>do</i> decide to commit violence typically are either at very/dangerously low satiety &amp; are thus likely to be desperate to get higher to avoid hurting their loved ones, or in possession of Hungers that drive/force them into committing violence even at high Satiety, whilst most Hunters, based on my understanding, have a general Morality that integrates &amp; works upon the basic/core assumption that monsters/supernatural beings don't count as enough of a person to count for the purposes of torture, murder or (in some cases for certain individuals of a particularly perverted or sick mindset) r^*e.<br /> <br /> Again, I've not read H:tV in any meaningful, lengthy and/or in-depth manner yet, so I could be off the mark on some things.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://www.nodiatis.com/personality.htm\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://www.nodiatis.com/pub/22.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25791-terrorcooper","name":"TerrorCooper","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"325"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-11-14T20:39:47-08:00","id":1525439,"title":"","content":"The things you find when you come to review for thoughts.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TyrannicalRabbit</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">And I know for what ideas I say this for, and to that benefit if another finds it does not cater to their presumption of demand, it doesn't move me a single bit.</div> </div> </div> </div>Do it in your own thread, then. I'm not the only person you're wasting the time of.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-11-16T07:11:09-08:00","id":1525508,"title":"","content":"It's a tough question without a definitive answer for several reasons. First off, the lack of &quot;fantasy filter&quot; may be a bit of a recurring theme but I don't know if we know how the individuals in question feel about Hunter specifically. I remember Mage being brought in as a point of contrast; what's the difference between casting fireball and dumping a can of gasoline and lighting a match? But it's a pretty small sample size to try extrapolating useful answers from.<br /> <br /> Second, broadening the point slightly, to what extent are we sure other gamelines do get a pass from the same people who viscerally dislike Beast? ChroD has a lot of gamelines and anecdotally I know plenty of people who like, say, Changeling and Vampire and probably couldn't reliably <i>name</i> all the other 9. Hunter's specific implementation &quot;getting a pass&quot; isn't a relevant question for people who haven't engaged with it in the first place. I'd only say I've read maybe a third to a half of them in enough depth to have a (even potentially) useful opinion. And of course there are people, even in this very thread, who also find gamelines besides Beast to be uncomfortable (vampire being the specific example, appropriately as it is often the most direct point of comparison).<br /> <br /> Bascially are we sure people who are coming to say they dislike Beast because of the lack of fantasy filter or for any other reason playing Hunter at all? Do we have avid Hunter players who are also vocal Beast critics?<br /> <br /> Anyway, as far as useful insight into why Beast rubs so many people the wrong way while Hunter specifically and other lines more broadly... don't, there are a couple reasons I can identify. The biggest one, I think, is that suffering is incidental to Hunter's goals, and central to Beast's. Now that's an oversimplification, certainly Hunter has its share of Ashwood Abbey sadists, but there's at least an argument to be made that <i>those people are doing it wrong</i>. Hunters may use innocent civilians as bait, may take unhealthy satisfaction in the pain they inflict on their targets, may even attack harmless members of the community because they didn't do their research before reaching for a gun. Hell, over a long enough career they almost certainly will do these things. But for Hunters this is cast as falling short of an ideal, this is a spiral into becoming as bad or worse than what you hunt. Beyond a certain threshold it's a fail state. In broad strokes this is true of many other splats as well.<br /> <br /> By contrast Beasts feed on the trauma directly. It's often an end in itself, and much harder to justify as a necessary means to some other, nobler end. A Beast causing as much trauma as possible is rewarded by a better feeding roll rather than punished with an integrity penalty. It can only be considered a failed goal if it draws unwanted attention and makes powerful enemies. That's not to say Beasts <i>can't </i>go through life trying to minimize trauma inflicted and maximize social good, but it's not the assumed success state and it feeds into my second point, which is that the core book examples lean heavily into the petty, malicious, and spiteful.<br /> <br /> Even after the rewrite toned a lot of things down (including changing Brent from 'young man' to 'frat boy', that very much did happen) so many of the Beasts presented come across as gleefully malevolent, doling out punishments disproportionate to the crime, committing entrapment, and generally failing to sell the necessity of their cruelty.<br /> <br /> It feels like a lot of punching down. It's one thing when a mob boss has his operation systematically dismantled by the vengeful ghost of a man he had killed, that kind of revenge arc is easy for a lot of people to get behind. A candy thief getting poisoned and strangled (even nonlethally) by an urban cryptid is, well, petty. It lacks even the karmic quality of the victims lacing their own candy with laxatives to get revenge the next time the thief strikes. I think this may be a problem of Beast being intended as the crossover splat but also trying to work as a standalone game. The examples end up heavily skewed toward terrorizing humans, who for the most part simply don't come across as meaningful threats to most Beasts. There's such a wide variety of other monsters in the setting to come to blows with, but the core can't rely on those while still providing a stand alone play experience.<br /> <br /> The Tyrant who feeds his hunger for power by tearing apart every huntsman that enters the Freehold he calls home and follows his buddies in the Summer Court to every protest march to gain satiety from Family Dinner when enemies aren't forthcoming is a viable concept, as is the Predator who plays the Most Dangerous Game with his werewolf companion to sharpen each other's skills as they defend the Gauntlet together. <br /> <br /> What I'm getting at is that Hunters are generally the underdogs and fighting dirty is what they have to do to win, and in terms of palatability Beasts come off a lot better when they're fighting other monsters in a sort of &quot;Godzilla establishing himself as king of all monsters&quot; kind of display. Because hey, Godzilla isn't usually something you want in your city, but sometimes he's better than the alternative.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5328-helmsderp","name":"HelmsDerp","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"652"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-11-16T19:44:10-08:00","id":1525555,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>HelmsDerp</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/1524397-hunters-pass-beasts-fail?p=1525508#post1525508\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Anyway, as far as useful insight into why Beast rubs so many people the wrong way while Hunter specifically and other lines more broadly... don't, there are a couple reasons I can identify. The biggest one, I think, is that suffering is incidental to Hunter's goals, and central to Beast's. Now that's an oversimplification, certainly Hunter has its share of Ashwood Abbey sadists, but there's at least an argument to be made that <i>those people are doing it wrong</i>. Hunters may use innocent civilians as bait, may take unhealthy satisfaction in the pain they inflict on their targets, may even attack harmless members of the community because they didn't do their research before reaching for a gun. Hell, over a long enough career they almost certainly will do these things. But for Hunters this is cast as falling short of an ideal, this is a spiral into becoming as bad or worse than what you hunt. Beyond a certain threshold it's a fail state. In broad strokes this is true of many other splats as well.<br /> <br /> By contrast Beasts feed on the trauma directly. It's often an end in itself, and much harder to justify as a necessary means to some other, nobler end. A Beast causing as much trauma as possible is rewarded by a better feeding roll rather than punished with an integrity penalty. It can only be considered a failed goal if it draws unwanted attention and makes powerful enemies. That's not to say Beasts <i>can't </i>go through life trying to minimize trauma inflicted and maximize social good, but it's not the assumed success state and it feeds into my second point, which is that the core book examples lean heavily into the petty, malicious, and spiteful.</div> </div> </div> </div>Springboarding off of some of this to connect a couple of things I've said in the past:<br /> <br /> 1. Looking around at the other gamelines, it's not difficult to see that Chronicles is a setting whose playable monsters tend to inhabit societies that at least mitigate if not necessarily negate the degree and likelihood of their degenerating into a state resembling the horror-folkloric basis of the monster whose name is on the box; werewolves who fall away from the Shadow tend to look more like movie werewolves than stewards of a spiritual world, the Sects give newly-Awakened mages the training to know better than to cast their way into alienating themselves from everyone they know and love (and making themselves and their surrounds very strange places to live besides), and the comparison of the Kindred and the Strix to pop-culture vampires and folklore vampires (and the relation of this dichotomy to the Humanity scale) is an old one.<br /> <br /> The splats for which socialization among their peers is not as commonly a net benefit tend to be the ones in which in-group paranoia is a necessary quality to possess (e.g. demons and their distrust of Agencies and more broadly each other) or the primary society is maladapted (e.g. the Arisen's headcount being largely cloistered among people who serve gods of suffering, have sworn loyalty to you, and are by default some of the only people who can touch you without seeing eternity), or things in the same overall genre.<br /> <br /> Another common point is that the failstates for these monsters tend to include &quot;getting too close to the source of their magic/having that source manifest through them too strongly&quot; \u2014 Deviant gets the most overt with this by directly correlating greater supernatural power with Instability and Scars, and similar states in other gamelines like the Enraptured or the Broken Souls evoke the same sort of playing-with-fire thematics as well as gesturing toward positive social connections and reduced reliance on personal supernatural power as the remedy to sliding down the slippery slope to soul-explosion et al. (There's a flip side to this, but it's part of Point 3.)<br /> <br /> The thing about most of those failstates is that they tend to be highly visible to the mortal populace in comparison to their more conspiratorially advantaged fellows, which in Beast's metaphysic means that in the absence of specific maneuvers to the contrary the Apex of a given hive is likely to be a powerful (and accordingly Hungry) monster without the support apparatus to hide their activities or feed themselves less noticeably. Which brings us to the next item:<br /> <br /> 2. The corebook and especially the Kickstarter draft leaned heavily toward presenting Beasts in the same vein as the other types of monster who become the Apex \u2014 high-Lair, low-Satiety monsters dancing on the edge of starvation to mainline their Atavisms and feed on less restrictive fare at the cost of being a missed step away from being almost completely alone \u2014 instead of monsters who inhabit a cosmopolitan supernatural world and experience a Kinship to most of it.<br /> <br /> The reasoning for this is fairly obvious \u2014 the incentives for Gorged characters are not nearly as actively driven as those for Starving characters, and despite the awkward nature of the patch-job to present a broad direction the Kickstarter draft definitely understood that you want to push characters to have goals they move towards \u2014 but the fact of the matter is that when you want to present a character type as associating with the supernatural (variously ingratiating themselves into local societies or piecemealing their own collection of misfits from the fringes) and having personal pursuits that are likely to cause trouble for themselves and the people close to them, it is generally advantageous to dedicate some space to exploring how that character type manages to be a functional member of those societies.<br /> <br /> The Player's Guide did a lot for this, including giving a very solid reason why the Beasts of a hive wouldn't want another Beast to be the Apex while also not aiming for the seat themselves, but even after the revisions the corebook still angled itself pretty heavily toward &quot;Beasts are the problem children of the magical world and they love making a mess of things&quot;; the only thing actually causing breaking points is good for in Beast is expanding the Lair (which you can only do once per story and which requires enough &quot;space&quot; to do so) and building up spiritual similarity between a location and one of your Chambers, and in both cases there's a less &quot;violent&quot; alternative in using Nightmares, but guess which one resonates more with &quot;one of the ways to achieve the only endgame that isn't an overt failure is to create a bunch of Chambers and make a show of outdoing your neighbors.&quot;<br /> <br /> I'd love for Beast to have more examples of fixers and figureheads and nightmare sorcerers and enigmatic minions and other occult cuckoo situations where Passing Resemblance can come into play and being Gorged lets you act stealthily in the shadow of other monsters by pretending to be a neonate or an initiate or fresh out of the Hedge while you make friends and study their bad sides, but Beast Core was more interested in the game where you're a big monster who fucks shit up, and that's laid an unfortunate weight on the scales of the game's perspective in combination with its general focus on the omnipresence of varying levels of malice in the world.<br /> <br /> 3. Being a social monster is, of course, not a pure positive in this setting either, as seen most directly by organized top-down structures like those favored by the Judges, the Exarchs, the God-Machine, many vampires, etc; look no further than the two gamelines for which &quot;conspiracy&quot; is a generalized mechanical term: Deviant and Hunter.<br /> <br /> The Devoted get to be villainous opposites to the Renegades on the strength of their Loyalty being to people who are part of an entity that wants to use them and their hateful Conviction being the aspect of their mentality that can more lastingly stabilize their occult condition; without a roaring fire of revenge to focus them, they keep themselves stable by constantly following directives set by the Principles of a secret organization that cracks open people's souls so magic powers come out (or makes use of those people for their magic powers), and working for or helping to create such an organization tends to actively undo a Renegade's marginally more positive transformation into a Guardian by association. Even the parts of the Web of Pain that are trying to do good have to work against the fact that it's easy to instrumentalize the Remade because they are no longer human and they need something to work toward in order to not melt.<br /> <br /> Hunter gets here on a different route: conspiracies are often contrasted with compacts as being top-down global structures instead of bottom-up regional alliances, with the former often being much older than the latter, with a variable relation of cause and effect tying that age to the conspiracy's access to supernatural resources. The plucky monster hunters of the tri-city area coming together to pool funds and share information may have to vet their allies more than they used to to keep an attentive mind-reader from tracking down the whole collection of names staying out of each other's way, but the Lucifuge and the Men In Black and the rest have Secrets to keep, and that means you go where they tell you to do and do what they tell you to do, and unless you had the good fortune to be assigned to work with your best buddy (and he's already on the payroll with you rather than being part of the cell you're embedded with) you're going to start having trouble maintaining personal relationships where you can confide in people \u2014 some of those groups can afford therapists in-the-know who are paid for their discretion, but conspiracies have finite headcounts.<br /> <br /> Other games have ways of growing a group's power without necessarily raising its membership's Supernatural Potency \u2014 werewolf packs generally can stop raising their Primal Urge past the third or fourth dot thanks to division of labor, totemic advantages, and the benefits of shapeshifting and spirit-magic, while krewes of the Bound can collect and create Mementos and Ceremonies to fill the gap in their Synergy and otherwise lean on collective action and pooled resources \u2014 but tough choices and difficult obligations tend to come with those increases of power as surely as any Ban or frailty, because working with the supernatural is occasionally wondrous but absolutely not a bed of roses.<br /> <br /> As may be inferred from how Family Dinner works (and the way Satiety tends to go down faster with higher Lair), the Begotten are no stranger to being part of groups that do some dirt. The main difference, of course, is that usually Beasts are operating in those groups to the end of establishing personal connections in mutual relation to their sustenance needs and/or higher goals \u2014 if you haven't been press-ganged into some monster's cult of personality, you're likely riding the line between &quot;I can stand to gain from helping this flesh-eating worm-lady consume local pets&quot; and &quot;the flesh-eating worm-lady and I can talk about problems we have as monsters without being shunned for our monstrous nature.&quot;<br /> <br /> The way Satiety Conditions work means most of the more fruitful such relationships are going to be a bit of a give-and-take, and while getting too full or too hungry is liable to result in hurt and rejection, the most comfortable position for a Kinship-focused Beast to be in is one for which the primary risk is negligence \u2014 as a heavily Resistance-oriented splat whose text suggests are inclined to fatalism, Beasts are no strangers to hurting and getting hurt, and while this by no means makes them exempt from having and trying to live up to ideals, it does tend to mean a lot of the ones that survive will have arrived at a certain level of &quot;cost of doing business&quot; thinking due to living in the headspace between rabid need and isolating numbness, broken up by the rush of id/ego-assertion from getting to be one of The Ones Who Eat (whether that comes with the ingroup validation of being around other Ones Who Eat or the bleaker but more singular head-trip of becoming <i>The</i> One Who Eats, for which see Point 1).<br /> <br /> Beast tends to paint mortals as victims, but it notably doesn't put a lot of wordcount toward painting the Begotten as thinking of humans as intrinsically beneath them the way certain stripes of other monster do; they know what people can do, and at any given time of their life the vicissitudes of feeding might mean they're not much better on most fronts, to say nothing of the lifetime of lessons they share with other former-human monsters from growing up as a person with Integrity in the Chronicles of Darkness.<br /> <br /> tl;dr Beast's entropic focus and general inclination toward stripping away the bullshit to get to the core runs across the difficulty that even if one wants to avoid giving spotlight to attractive bullshit for thematic reasons, it is still to the text's advantage to present ways in which the ugly truth is worthwhile to pay attention to \u2014 the value of catharsis (even just in the release of a character understanding that This Was Always Going To Happen) and from finding comfort and taking victories where you can (even among monsters who don't make you feel alienated from the world or from taking the president of the HOA down a peg) would be a better pull than the more aestheticized tilt towards Big Powerful Monster Smash.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-01-16T21:22:38-08:00","id":1557700,"title":"","content":"Going by how the book describes it, merely giving your prey a good spook isn't enough. Beasts don't feed on fear, they feed on terror. They need to be shaking in their boots by the time the scene ends.<br /> <br /> Part of the Teaching Lessons process involves scaring your victim to such a degree, that they experience an epiphany about their life. Not unlike how Jigsaw forces people to compete in death games to teach them how valuable life is.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> A Collector teaches people not to place so much value on material possessions.<br /> <br /> <br /> A Destroyer teaches people that they aren't nearly as safe as they think they are.<br /> <br /> <br /> A Predator teaches people they are not at the top of the food chain.<br /> <br /> <br /> And so on.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/29009-darkking","name":"DarkKing","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar29009_1.png","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2025"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"639"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-01-16T21:56:10-08:00","id":1557704,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DarkKing</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/1524397-hunters-pass-beasts-fail?p=1557700#post1557700\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Going by how the book describes it, merely giving your prey a good spook isn't enough. Beasts don't feed on fear, they feed on terror. They need to be shaking in their boots by the time the scene ends.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's explicitly untrue. Low Satiety meals can be as simple as a mugging, a forceful warning, or namedropping to get out of a traffic ticket.<br /> <br /> The Horror can go ham in nightmares, particularly if it feeds deeply, but the shock feeding in material reality inflicts is specifically not required to be at the level of a breaking point or otherwise substantially worse than a changeling harvesting Glamour from emotions.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1},{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}